10:46:00 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Your respiratory system does not directly “test the air” before you breathe. Instead, it monitors the gases in your blood and adjusts breathing based on that. - **Main signal: carbon dioxide (CO₂).** As your cells use oxygen, they produce CO₂. Rising CO₂ makes the blood more acidic. Sensors in the brainstem and major arteries detect this change and tell your breathing muscles to breathe faster or deeper. - **Oxygen sensors provide a backup/important low-oxygen signal.** Special sensors called **peripheral chemoreceptors**, located mainly in the carotid arteries in your neck and the aorta near the heart, measure oxygen levels in arterial blood. If oxygen becomes too low—such as at high altitude or with lung disease—they strongly stimulate breathing. - **Brainstem controls the rhythm.** Breathing centers in the brainstem combine these signals and automatically control the diaphragm and other breathing muscles. Normally, air contains about **21% oxygen**, which is plenty. Even if oxygen in the air decreases somewhat, your body can compensate by breathing more deeply or rapidly. At very high altitude or in enclosed spaces where oxygen is low and CO₂ may build up, those blood-gas sensors trigger stronger breathing responses—but if oxygen drops too severely or too quickly, the system can be overwhelmed.
