10:46:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: In normal life, you are mostly right: your body continuously monitors whether your blood is getting enough oxygen, and it usually adjusts breathing long before oxygen becomes dangerously low. But the key distinction is: - It monitors **oxygen delivered to the blood**, not the air in the room directly. - It also relies heavily on **CO₂ levels** to control ordinary breathing. - Normal environments have a large oxygen safety margin, so random fainting from low oxygen is rare. If your lungs are working normally and you enter a mildly stuffy room, your blood oxygen usually stays normal. You may feel uncomfortable because of heat, humidity, odors, or elevated CO₂, but you are not usually close to passing out. There *are* situations where low oxygen can cause little warning—especially when oxygen falls slowly, at high altitude, or when another gas displaces oxygen in an enclosed space. People can become confused or faint without feeling dramatically short of breath. That is why “I feel okay” is not a reliable safety test in potentially oxygen-poor environments. So: the body has oxygen-sensing and self-regulation, and it works very well in ordinary conditions—but it is not a perfect instant detector of whether surrounding air is safe.
